WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.625
[CHOIR SINGING]

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:03.960
CHOIR: Ooh, ooh, ooh.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.940
Ooh, ooh, ooh.

00:00:09.405 --> 00:00:27.944
If my true love, she was
near me, she was near me,

00:00:27.944 --> 00:00:35.928
I will never find another.

00:00:43.413 --> 00:00:44.940
LARRY ARNN: I had
this introduction

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:48.450
of Professor Jackson worked
out for last time, when

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:49.680
he was scheduled to speak.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.380
And when he couldn't come, RJ
Pestritto filled in for him

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:53.630
beautifully.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.657
And he couldn't come
because Gina had given him

00:00:56.657 --> 00:00:58.740
another baby, and they
were going to bring it home

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.490
from the hospital.

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:01.380
And he wouldn't miss that.

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:03.321
I guess he thought
Gina might drop it.

00:01:03.321 --> 00:01:06.550
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:07.960
And my introduction
last time was

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:09.560
that I was going
to use the occasion

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:11.970
to settle the dispute
that Gina and I have.

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:14.660
Is Gina here?

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.467
She's going to come some time.

00:01:16.467 --> 00:01:17.636
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:17.636 --> 00:01:19.010
But her presence
is here in a way

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.982
that I'm about to
make known to you.

00:01:21.982 --> 00:01:24.440
Because she and I have been
arguing for years what he looks

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.180
like.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.252
She claims he looks like a hobo.

00:01:28.252 --> 00:01:29.490
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:32.300
And I claim that he looks
like Rasputin, the mad monk.

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.510
[LAUGHTER]

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:38.150
Or else he looks like the
great uncle of Raymond Spiotta.

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:41.552
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.120
But I have to recite his resume.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.590
And the question
of his appearance

00:01:54.590 --> 00:01:59.030
begins with the first award
this man has ever received,

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:01.880
which I'm now going
to show to you.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:08.090
He was the calendar
boy for the Big Boy

00:02:08.090 --> 00:02:09.979
Market in Modesto, California.

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:13.437
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:02:27.790 --> 00:02:31.960
And you can all
thank Gina for this.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:34.030 --> 00:02:36.970
And lest this should
ever be forgotten,

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:39.310
there are about 200
copies of this photograph

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.882
back there for any of you who
want them to take with them.

00:02:41.882 --> 00:02:43.494
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:43.494 --> 00:02:45.660
Because I think it important
for them to get around.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.992
[LAUGHTER]

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:54.730
Justin Jackson is
a very unusual man.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:58.660
He comes from Modesto,
see, calendar boy.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:01.240
What does he do?

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:04.000
Medieval literature.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:06.490
He knows Old and Middle English.

00:03:06.490 --> 00:03:08.470
He probably knows
all the literature

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:11.920
we have from it nearly by heart.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:14.980
He is this kind of a teacher.

00:03:14.980 --> 00:03:18.640
He has broken down the
parts of the teaching art

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:21.340
into its component parts.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:25.540
And he is beloved by
all of the students

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.950
to whom he so
consistently gives Cs.

00:03:27.950 --> 00:03:31.930
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.390
I had the pleasure of a
two-hour conversation with him.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:36.760
It probably wasn't
pleasurable to him,

00:03:36.760 --> 00:03:38.590
but it was so much fun to me.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:43.140
Because he pours
forth how to do it.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:47.410
And he can state exactly what
he wants them to be able to do.

00:03:47.410 --> 00:03:50.860
They must learn to
analyze literature.

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:55.150
And that means they must get
inside it and see what it means

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:59.980
and why it means that and how it
expresses the meaning of that.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:02.110
And then they must,
having understood

00:04:02.110 --> 00:04:05.920
that, that is to say,
learned to read that way,

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:07.420
they must be able
to present that

00:04:07.420 --> 00:04:13.750
in an organized and clear
and concise piece of writing.

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:17.019
And that is such a difficult
thing to learn how to do.

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:19.959
And if you,
prospective students,

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:23.140
learn how to do that thing,
you will be a different person

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:26.340
than anything else
could make you into be.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.320
And one of the few
best people I have ever

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:32.470
seen at the teaching
of how to do that thing

00:04:32.470 --> 00:04:34.948
is our beloved Justin Jackson.

00:04:34.948 --> 00:04:38.434
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:52.670
JUSTIN JACKSON: Thank you.

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:55.865
Congratulations, Jim--
much, much deserved.

00:04:59.170 --> 00:05:04.180
You become responsible forever
for what you have tamed--

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:09.310
some thoughts on roses,
foxes, and friendship.

00:05:09.310 --> 00:05:13.690
President Arnn, my wonderful,
beloved colleagues, students

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:17.110
and guests, I'm honored to
have been asked to speak

00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:19.840
to you today at convocation.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.445
And I say honored
and not delighted.

00:05:21.445 --> 00:05:24.620
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:27.460
Not because he's
sitting to my right.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:30.370
To be perfectly
honest, I'm really

00:05:30.370 --> 00:05:33.640
ill-equipped to give
a talk like this.

00:05:33.640 --> 00:05:37.840
I have not the
eloquence of Dr. Birzer,

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:43.150
the wit of Dr. Whalen, the
decency nor the grace of Dr.

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:48.580
Wyatt-Hayes nor the majestic,
sonorous voice of Dr. Conner.

00:05:48.580 --> 00:05:52.460
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.635
I like to think I'm
as funny as Dr. Arnn.

00:05:54.635 --> 00:05:57.896
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.242
LARRY ARNN: Funny looking.

00:06:03.242 --> 00:06:04.910
JUSTIN JACKSON: But
those sorts of jokes

00:06:04.910 --> 00:06:07.284
get you fired if you're not
the president of the college.

00:06:07.284 --> 00:06:10.206
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:17.580
The birth of my son Simeon got
me out of the last address,

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:20.140
so I thought I was in the clear.

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:22.890
But obviously, I was wrong.

00:06:22.890 --> 00:06:26.430
I've tried diligently all
week to get out of this.

00:06:26.430 --> 00:06:27.240
I really, I have.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:28.650
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:30.960
My whole family has been
wiped out and in bed

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.290
for the past week with
Strep, and running

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:37.155
103-degree temperatures.

00:06:37.155 --> 00:06:38.280
I had hoped to be infected.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:41.526
[LAUGHTER]

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:53.380
My daughters thought it rather
strange, though entertaining,

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:55.740
that I kept licking
their hands and faces--

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:59.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:17.770
--mumbling, give me those
germs, and dabbing my cheeks

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:19.180
with their used tissues.

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:21.310
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:24.850
Yesterday, I actually offered
Dr. [? Sundal ?] 10 bucks

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:26.050
to hit me with his car.

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:29.515
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:32.990 --> 00:07:36.020
But he declined,
indicating that he

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:38.255
was worried about putting
a dent in his precious.

00:07:38.255 --> 00:07:41.150
[LAUGHTER]

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:43.160
I thought that was rather sweet.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.136
He's never called me
his precious before.

00:07:45.136 --> 00:07:48.608
[LAUGHTER AND APPLAUSE]

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:00.930
Nevertheless, I am here.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:03.330
And really, not only am I
honored to be here today,

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:06.060
I am happy, happy to be
here with my colleagues

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.400
and friends, of
course, but especially

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.380
happy to see you seniors.

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:12.570
I have grown close
to many of you

00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:14.820
and have witnessed
your intellectual

00:08:14.820 --> 00:08:17.640
and spiritual
maturation, and consider

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:21.930
myself fortunate for
having studied under you.

00:08:21.930 --> 00:08:24.510
When I was asked to
give this address,

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:27.360
my first question to Dr.
Blackstock was simple.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.910
What are we looking for
in this sort of talk?

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:32.845
Dr. Blackstock suggested
that I offer something

00:08:32.845 --> 00:08:34.470
that would take us
away from the hustle

00:08:34.470 --> 00:08:37.260
and bustle of our daily
activities, especially

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:40.470
at this hectic time of the
year, that perhaps I could

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:43.350
look to something uplifting.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:47.190
But I imagine that the most
uplifting thing I could offer

00:08:47.190 --> 00:08:48.840
would be simply to
point out the fact

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:52.680
that we are here in a moment
carved out for us already

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:56.070
from the hustle and bustle,
a place where we find

00:08:56.070 --> 00:08:59.250
the friends with whom
we spend our time

00:08:59.250 --> 00:09:03.120
and waste our time, the
professor, then the students,

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:06.550
from whom we all learn so much.

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:09.810
So here we are again
at the end of the year,

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:11.640
saying goodbye to
friends with whom

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:13.920
we've just become acquainted.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:16.060
At freshman
convocation, Dr. Arnn

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:19.860
often reminds parents of
their bittersweet moment,

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:23.760
what they've waited, worked, and
hoped for for their children.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.240
This new life at college,
this foray into adulthood,

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:30.500
has finally come to pass.

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.560
These parents have cultivated,
nourished their children,

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:36.200
and now they must give
them up, offer them

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:39.680
to us to continue this project.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:42.830
These parents must
now say goodbye.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:46.794
Today, many of us here
feel the same way.

00:09:46.794 --> 00:09:48.710
We have come to know you
and to know ourselves

00:09:48.710 --> 00:09:51.230
better because of you.

00:09:51.230 --> 00:09:53.480
And now you must leave us.

00:09:53.480 --> 00:09:54.425
And this makes me sad.

00:09:57.150 --> 00:10:00.506
We humans are a
funny lot, aren't we?

00:10:00.506 --> 00:10:02.130
There's something in
us that can't help

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:07.290
but to affirm and participate
in the goodness of life,

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:11.040
even when a participation in
that goodness, in that beauty,

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.815
will ultimately lead to sadness.

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:20.220
I often like to tell my
students about my daughter

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:23.520
Lydia and her first
exposure to death.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.610
Her goldfish died.

00:10:26.610 --> 00:10:29.970
I would like to say
that it was a sad day.

00:10:29.970 --> 00:10:34.200
But Lydia didn't quite
get death at that point.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.120
She slowly came
to the realization

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:40.710
that her fish wasn't going to
be swimming any longer, that she

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:44.850
would no longer have to feed
him because he was dead.

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:51.370
When we walked outside to
give her fish a proper burial,

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:55.390
she turned to me and asked,
Dada, if a fish dies,

00:10:55.390 --> 00:10:58.055
does that mean birds die?

00:10:58.055 --> 00:10:58.930
We don't have a bird.

00:10:58.930 --> 00:10:59.965
She just likes birds.

00:10:59.965 --> 00:11:02.770
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:02.770 --> 00:11:03.550
Yes, sweetheart.

00:11:03.550 --> 00:11:05.370
Birds die.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:07.690
Do cats die?

00:11:07.690 --> 00:11:09.250
I knew where this was going.

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:12.010
Yes, sweetheart, cats die.

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:13.600
Even Floyd?

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:15.430
This is our cat.

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:16.880
Yes.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:20.470
And if Floyd dies, does
that mean Puck will die?

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:21.660
He's our dog with one eye.

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:25.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:27.660
You can see the clear
hierarchy of pets here.

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:29.740
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:33.830
Fish, bird, cat, dog.

00:11:33.830 --> 00:11:36.160
Lydia's my budding
little Aristotelean.

00:11:40.060 --> 00:11:42.100
Matilda, too, Dada?

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:43.660
This is our dog with two eyes.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:46.999
[LAUGHTER]

00:11:51.770 --> 00:11:53.500
Yes, sweetheart.

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:54.810
You're going to die, Dada?

00:11:57.340 --> 00:11:59.300
Yes, sweetheart, Daddy will die.

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:03.500
And if you die, will Mommy die?

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:06.730
And I thought, hey, at least
I rate higher than the dogs.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:10.090
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:16.130
Yes, sweetheart, Mommy will die.

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:19.100
And will I die, Dada?

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:22.040
See how much fun parenthood is.

00:12:22.040 --> 00:12:25.190
Yes, sweetheart, you will die.

00:12:25.190 --> 00:12:29.390
I tried to explain that, though
we die, we don't really die.

00:12:29.390 --> 00:12:32.750
But this explanation
didn't really interest her.

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:35.480
Lydia looked down at the grass
as we walked, took a few more

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:39.350
steps, looked back and asked,
can I have another fish?

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:42.750
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:46.590
And with this single question,
the pronouncement of her desire

00:12:46.590 --> 00:12:50.100
for the responsibility to
feed a goldfish, to name it,

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:53.880
to watch it swim, to watch
it die, and to bury it,

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:57.900
with all of this, my
three-year-old simultaneously

00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:00.660
rejected the absurdity
and meaninglessness

00:13:00.660 --> 00:13:03.090
of death and
suffering and affirmed

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:07.770
the beauty of God's creation,
affirmed and accepted

00:13:07.770 --> 00:13:12.330
our vocation, indeed,
life itself, a life

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:15.120
of responsibility, and
with that responsibility,

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:16.860
a vulnerability.

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:19.560
For a brief moment,
a very brief moment,

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:22.530
she stood at the
crossroads of nihilism,

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:25.650
to see humans as simply
beings unto death,

00:13:25.650 --> 00:13:27.930
that all is, in fact, vanity.

00:13:27.930 --> 00:13:30.720
And she rejected it, and
wished to accept her role

00:13:30.720 --> 00:13:34.140
as a person who needs to
be responsible for others,

00:13:34.140 --> 00:13:37.920
a person who needs
to feed a fish.

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:42.960
The title of my talk, a line
taken from the book, The Little

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:47.010
Prince, "You Become
Responsible Forever for What

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:51.240
You Have Tamed," seemed
rather appropriate today.

00:13:51.240 --> 00:13:52.730
As I look out into
the audience, I

00:13:52.730 --> 00:13:56.130
see only an affirmation of
what it means to be human.

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:59.970
I see students for whom we
as faculty are responsible,

00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:01.470
students who came
to this college

00:14:01.470 --> 00:14:05.130
to have their minds
cultivated, their souls tamed.

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.470
And this is really true.

00:14:07.470 --> 00:14:11.580
When you enrolled at Hillsdale
College, you asked to be tamed.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:14.310
You came to our classes,
to our office hours,

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:17.750
and requested, please tame me.

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:20.810
And we are obliged happily.

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:25.250
To our guests here today,
a bearded, unkempt,

00:14:25.250 --> 00:14:28.430
ogre-ish looking man, declaring
that we have tamed these

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:30.440
students must seem--

00:14:30.440 --> 00:14:35.730
[LAUGHTER]

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:40.760
--must seem hubristic, at best,
and coldly despotic, at worst.

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:46.330
But here is the secret
so many of these students

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:49.810
and certainly, all of my
colleagues, have discovered.

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:52.810
In the act of taming
someone about whom we care,

00:14:52.810 --> 00:14:55.330
we, too, become tamed.

00:14:55.330 --> 00:14:58.240
The act of taming
implies not domination,

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:01.300
not some sort of
condescending didacticism,

00:15:01.300 --> 00:15:05.320
but an act of responsibility,
of self-giving a vulnerability

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:07.380
for both parties.

00:15:07.380 --> 00:15:09.550
And as much as we try
to help shape the minds

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:13.180
and souls of our students,
so are our minds and souls

00:15:13.180 --> 00:15:15.694
shaped by them.

00:15:15.694 --> 00:15:17.110
I'm willing to
argue that there is

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:19.870
an intellectual and spiritual
give-and-take going on

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:24.250
in office after office on
this campus on a daily basis.

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:27.820
A student comes to the office
of the professor with a paper,

00:15:27.820 --> 00:15:30.670
her thoughts on
Aristotle and CS Lewis,

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:32.980
perhaps even her
deeply held beliefs,

00:15:32.980 --> 00:15:35.890
a paper which contains, not
simply clever thoughts strung

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:39.310
together, not simply
footnotes to show she's read

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:41.680
the relevant literature,
but something

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:44.230
of the student's
essential being, something

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:47.590
invisible to the eye, but
something more real, more

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:50.920
palpable than the paper on which
the student has communicated

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:52.790
her thoughts.

00:15:52.790 --> 00:15:55.840
She has learned in some
way how to think from us.

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:59.710
Having been tamed or
perhaps still being tamed--

00:15:59.710 --> 00:16:02.560
aren't we all-- she offers
something back to us

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:06.730
when she hands us the paper,
not our thoughts regurgitated,

00:16:06.730 --> 00:16:09.160
but our thoughts
appropriated, taken on

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:12.420
by her own experience,
her own thoughts,

00:16:12.420 --> 00:16:16.390
and communicated back to
us through her own words.

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:19.690
She offers us something outside
of ourselves back to us,

00:16:19.690 --> 00:16:21.530
a gift.

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:25.190
Perhaps she even offers her
very personhood back to us.

00:16:25.190 --> 00:16:28.190
She tames us, for
she has actually been

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:31.790
responsible for us all along.

00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:33.800
This is perhaps the
very secret many

00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:35.600
of you now must
understand as you're

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:38.870
about to leave this campus--

00:16:38.870 --> 00:16:43.550
to get a job, to get married,
and, horror of horrors,

00:16:43.550 --> 00:16:46.554
to have children.

00:16:46.554 --> 00:16:47.970
All of this time,
when you thought

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:50.070
you lived under the
thumb of your parents

00:16:50.070 --> 00:16:53.010
as they tried to direct you
in your various endeavors,

00:16:53.010 --> 00:16:56.160
be it by rules or
by finances, they

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:59.280
were trying to tame
your young spirit.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:00.960
Well, you were right
in thinking this.

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:03.280
This is exactly what
they were trying to do.

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:06.540
They were taming
you, directing you.

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:08.819
But here's what
you may not know.

00:17:08.819 --> 00:17:11.760
You've always had
the upper hand.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.880
You were never the servant
in this relationship.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:17.369
You were always the master.

00:17:17.369 --> 00:17:19.260
You were being tamed,
but in doing so,

00:17:19.260 --> 00:17:23.020
your parents offered
themselves as a gift to you.

00:17:23.020 --> 00:17:24.660
And now it's your turn.

00:17:24.660 --> 00:17:27.599
You are now the ones who must
go out and do the taming.

00:17:27.599 --> 00:17:30.330
And yet, always remain
mindful that the first gesture

00:17:30.330 --> 00:17:33.000
of taming, the first
true act of love,

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:36.570
is vulnerability, the
willingness to suffer,

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:40.680
a recognition that you,
too, will be tamed.

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:44.130
And this is what it
means to be human.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:46.440
I hope everyone will
forgive me for this,

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:50.010
but today I'm going to read
from The Little Prince.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:52.210
It's a wonderful
little children's book,

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:55.620
and seems apropos for today.

00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:58.920
If you are interested in an
investigation of the ontology

00:17:58.920 --> 00:18:01.890
of love as a phenomenological
occurrence that

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:04.860
ruptures the horizon
of Heideggerian being,

00:18:04.860 --> 00:18:08.400
and opens up the infinitude
of a self-emptying, keynoitic,

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:13.360
hypostatic instantiation of
a theandric existence proper,

00:18:13.360 --> 00:18:15.060
then this children's
book is for you.

00:18:15.060 --> 00:18:18.469
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:25.130
But today, I simply
want to focus

00:18:25.130 --> 00:18:28.007
on a scene when the Little
Prince encounters a fox

00:18:28.007 --> 00:18:29.840
and learns a little
something about his love

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:34.580
for his rose back on his
home planet, Asteroid B 612.

00:18:34.580 --> 00:18:37.100
I simply wish to
read this to you,

00:18:37.100 --> 00:18:40.280
and will resist the urge,
perhaps for once in my life,

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:42.588
to comment on this
piece of literature.

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:49.920
"The Little Prince asked,
what does that mean, tame?

00:18:49.920 --> 00:18:53.760
It is an act too often
neglected, said the Fox.

00:18:53.760 --> 00:18:56.370
It means to establish ties.

00:18:56.370 --> 00:18:57.870
To establish ties?

00:18:57.870 --> 00:18:59.790
Just that, said the Fox.

00:18:59.790 --> 00:19:01.230
To me, you are
still nothing more

00:19:01.230 --> 00:19:05.450
than a little boy who is just
like 100,000 other little boys.

00:19:05.450 --> 00:19:07.850
And I have no need of you.

00:19:07.850 --> 00:19:10.440
And you, on your part,
have no need of me.

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:14.640
To you, I am nothing more than
a fox like 100,000 other foxes.

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:17.940
But if you tame me, then
we shall need each other.

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:20.520
To me, you will be
unique in all the world.

00:19:20.520 --> 00:19:23.790
To you, I shall be
unique in all the world.

00:19:23.790 --> 00:19:27.210
I am beginning to understand,
said the Little Prince.

00:19:27.210 --> 00:19:28.410
There is a flower.

00:19:28.410 --> 00:19:31.980
I think that she has tamed me.

00:19:31.980 --> 00:19:35.760
The Fox gazed at the Little
Prince for a long time.

00:19:35.760 --> 00:19:38.310
Please tame me, he said.

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:41.070
I want to very much, the
Little Prince replied.

00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:42.720
But I have not much time.

00:19:42.720 --> 00:19:45.120
I have friends to discover
and a great many things

00:19:45.120 --> 00:19:47.400
to understand.

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:52.350
One only understands the things
that one tames, said the Fox.

00:19:52.350 --> 00:19:55.560
Men have no more time
to understand anything.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:58.530
They buy things already
made at the shops,

00:19:58.530 --> 00:20:02.160
but there is no shop anywhere
where one can buy friendship.

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:04.680
And so men have no
friends anymore.

00:20:04.680 --> 00:20:07.590
If you want a friend, tame me.

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:10.470
So the Little Prince
tamed the Fox.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:12.000
And when the hour
of his departure

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:17.840
drew near, aah, said
the Fox, I shall cry.

00:20:17.840 --> 00:20:20.750
It is your own fault,
said the Little Prince.

00:20:20.750 --> 00:20:23.070
I never wished you
any sort of harm,

00:20:23.070 --> 00:20:25.760
but you wanted me to tame you.

00:20:25.760 --> 00:20:27.890
Yes, that is so, said the Fox.

00:20:27.890 --> 00:20:31.430
But now you are going to
cry, said the Little Prince.

00:20:31.430 --> 00:20:34.100
Yes, that is so, said the Fox.

00:20:34.100 --> 00:20:36.710
Then it has done
you no good at all.

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:39.200
It has done me
good, said the Fox,

00:20:39.200 --> 00:20:41.540
because the color
of the wheat fields.

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:44.570
And then he added, go
and look at the roses.

00:20:44.570 --> 00:20:46.520
You will understand now."

00:20:46.520 --> 00:20:47.390
I can't-- I'm sorry.

00:20:47.390 --> 00:20:49.000
I can't read The Little
Prince without showing

00:20:49.000 --> 00:20:49.880
you the pictures.

00:20:49.880 --> 00:20:51.950
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:51.950 --> 00:20:53.390
He took so long to draw them.

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:58.350
"That yours is unique
in all the world.

00:20:58.350 --> 00:21:00.540
Then come back to
say goodbye to me,

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:02.910
and I will make you a
present of a secret.

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:09.710
The Little Prince went away
to look again at the roses.

00:21:09.710 --> 00:21:12.200
You are not at all
like my rose, he said.

00:21:12.200 --> 00:21:13.760
As yet, you are nothing.

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:17.150
No one has tamed you, and
you have tamed to no one.

00:21:17.150 --> 00:21:19.850
You were like my Fox
when I first knew him.

00:21:19.850 --> 00:21:22.820
He was only a Fox like
100,000 other foxes,

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:24.740
but I made him my friend.

00:21:24.740 --> 00:21:28.040
And now he is unique
in all the world.

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:30.650
And the roses were
very much embarrassed.

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:32.930
You are beautiful, but
you're empty, he went on.

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:35.420
One could not die for you.

00:21:35.420 --> 00:21:37.250
To be sure, an
ordinary passerby would

00:21:37.250 --> 00:21:39.530
think that my rose
looked just like you,

00:21:39.530 --> 00:21:41.720
the rose that belongs to me.

00:21:41.720 --> 00:21:43.880
But in herself alone,
she is more important

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:46.340
than all the hundreds
of you other roses.

00:21:46.340 --> 00:21:48.350
Because it is she
that I have watered.

00:21:48.350 --> 00:21:51.020
Because it is she that I have
put under the glass globe.

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:54.266
Because it is she that I have
sheltered behind the screen.

00:21:54.266 --> 00:21:55.640
Because it is for
her that I have

00:21:55.640 --> 00:21:58.220
killed the caterpillars,
except the two or three

00:21:58.220 --> 00:22:00.800
that we saved to
become butterflies.

00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:02.570
Because it is she
that I have listened

00:22:02.570 --> 00:22:06.140
to when she grumbled or
boasted, or even sometimes

00:22:06.140 --> 00:22:07.580
when she said nothing.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:09.540
Because she is my rose.

00:22:09.540 --> 00:22:12.040
And he went back
to meet the Fox.

00:22:12.040 --> 00:22:13.520
Good-bye, he said.

00:22:13.520 --> 00:22:15.230
Good-bye, said the Fox.

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:18.920
And now here is my secret,
a very simple secret.

00:22:18.920 --> 00:22:21.980
It is only with the heart
that one can see rightly.

00:22:21.980 --> 00:22:24.980
What is essential is
invisible to the eye.

00:22:24.980 --> 00:22:26.930
What is essential is
invisible to the eye,

00:22:26.930 --> 00:22:28.820
the Little Prince
repeated, so that he

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:30.860
would be sure to remember.

00:22:30.860 --> 00:22:34.040
It is the time you have wasted
for your rose that makes

00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:36.080
your rose so important.

00:22:36.080 --> 00:22:37.670
It is the time I
have wasted for my

00:22:37.670 --> 00:22:39.336
rose, said the Little
Prince, so that he

00:22:39.336 --> 00:22:41.060
would be sure to remember.

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:44.120
Men have forgotten this
truth, said the Fox.

00:22:44.120 --> 00:22:46.010
But you must not forget it.

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:49.220
You become responsible forever
for what you have tamed.

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:52.280
You are responsible
for your rose.

00:22:52.280 --> 00:22:56.210
I am responsible for my rose,
the Little Prince repeated,

00:22:56.210 --> 00:22:58.850
so that he would be
sure to remember."

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:01.850
And so, my dear seniors, I
want to thank you for the time

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:03.500
we have wasted together.

00:23:03.500 --> 00:23:05.120
It has been a pleasure.

00:23:05.120 --> 00:23:08.470
[APPLAUSE]

